-- Q1: medium / L3 — Credit ordering rules (nonrefundable before refundable)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Torres, a single filer, has pre-credit tax liability of $3,500. She qualifies for a $4,000 nonrefundable Foreign Tax Credit and a $2,500 refundable American Opportunity Tax Credit (40% refundable). Applying the credit ordering rules, determine Torres's net tax outcome.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 tax liability and $1,000 refund", "$0 tax liability and $500 refund", "$0 tax liability and $0 refund", "$500 tax liability and $0 refund"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 26, nonrefundable credits are applied first, reducing tax liability to zero but not below. Torres's $4,000 FTC reduces her $3,500 liability to $0 (the excess $500 is lost unless carried forward). The refundable AOTC ($1,000 refundable portion) then generates a $1,000 refund since her liability is already zero. Wrong (B): This incorrectly limits the refundable AOTC to $500, but the 40% refundable portion of $2,500 is $1,000. Wrong (C): This ignores that the refundable portion of the AOTC can generate a refund even when liability is zero. Wrong (D): This incorrectly applies the refundable credit first, reducing liability before the nonrefundable credit.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Other Dependent Credit ($500 for non-CTC dependents)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Brennan and Liu (MFJ, AGI $180,000) claim three dependents: a 12-year-old daughter, a 19-year-old son who is a full-time college student, and Liu's 72-year-old father who lives with them. Under IRC Section 24, what is the family's total credit for dependents?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$2,000", "$3,000", "$4,000", "$6,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 24, the $2,000 Child Tax Credit applies only to qualifying children under age 17. The 12-year-old qualifies for $2,000. The 19-year-old son and 72-year-old father do not qualify for the CTC but each qualifies for the $500 Other Dependent Credit, totaling $1,000. The family's combined credit is $3,000. Wrong (A): This counts only the CTC for the 12-year-old and ignores the Other Dependent Credit for the other two dependents. Wrong (C): This incorrectly gives the 19-year-old son a full $2,000 CTC despite being over age 16. Wrong (D): This incorrectly gives all three dependents the full $2,000 CTC.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Premium Tax Credit basics
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 36B, which of the following describes the Premium Tax Credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A nonrefundable credit for employer-sponsored health insurance premiums", "A deduction for self-employed health insurance costs", "A refundable credit for health insurance purchased through a government marketplace", "A nonrefundable credit available only to taxpayers age 65 and older"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 36B, the Premium Tax Credit is a refundable credit available to taxpayers who purchase health insurance through a government Health Insurance Marketplace. Eligibility is based on household income falling between 100% and 400% of the federal poverty level. Wrong (A): The PTC applies to marketplace insurance, not employer-sponsored coverage, and it is refundable, not nonrefundable. Wrong (B): The self-employed health insurance deduction is an above-the-line deduction under Section 162(l), not a credit. Wrong (D): The PTC has no age restriction and is available to qualifying taxpayers of any age.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — FTC simplified election vs Form 1116
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Dunn (single) receives $8,000 in foreign dividends from a broadly diversified mutual fund, with $240 in foreign taxes withheld. She also has $2,000 of foreign-source royalty income (general category) with $600 in foreign taxes paid. Her total U.S. tax liability is $18,000 on $120,000 worldwide income. Dunn wants to minimize compliance burden. Evaluate whether Dunn qualifies for the simplified foreign tax credit election under IRC Section 904(j), and determine the optimal filing strategy.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Claim the simplified election for all $840 in foreign taxes without filing Form 1116", "Claim the simplified election for the $240 dividend withholding only and file Form 1116 for the $600 royalty taxes", "File Form 1116 for both categories because the royalty income disqualifies the simplified election entirely", "Deduct all $840 as an itemized deduction on Schedule A because it exceeds the simplified election threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 904(j), the simplified election allows taxpayers to claim a foreign tax credit without Form 1116 only for qualified passive category income (e.g., dividends, interest) when creditable foreign taxes do not exceed $300 ($600 MFJ). Dunn's $240 in passive dividend withholding qualifies. However, the $600 in general category royalty taxes requires Form 1116 with a separate basket limitation. She should use the simplified election for the dividends and file Form 1116 for the royalties. Wrong (A): The simplified election cannot be used for general category income such as royalties — only passive category income qualifies. Wrong (C): The royalty income does not disqualify the simplified election for the passive dividend income; the two categories are independent. Wrong (D): The foreign tax credit almost always provides greater benefit than the deduction, and the $840 total does not trigger any threshold that forces a deduction.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Child/Dependent Care Credit qualifying individual age limit
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Lawson pays $5,000 in after-school care expenses for her 14-year-old son so she can work full-time. Her AGI is $40,000. What is Lawson's Child and Dependent Care Credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0", "$600", "$1,000", "$1,050"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 21, the Child and Dependent Care Credit requires the qualifying child to be under age 13. Lawson's son is 14, so no expenses qualify for the credit regardless of the amount paid or her income level. Wrong (B): This incorrectly applies a 20% credit rate to a reduced expense amount, but no credit is available because the child exceeds the age limit. Wrong (C): This applies a percentage to the maximum $3,000 expense limit, but the child's age disqualifies the expense entirely. Wrong (D): This applies the 21% rate to $5,000, ignoring both the age requirement and the $3,000 single-child expense cap.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — EITC earned income definition
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Okafor, a single filer age 40 with no qualifying children, has the following 2026 income: $3,000 in interest from savings accounts, $5,000 in rental income from a duplex, and $8,000 in unemployment compensation. Her AGI is $16,000. Is Okafor eligible for the Earned Income Tax Credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because her AGI is below the EITC threshold for a single filer with no children", "No, because she has no earned income from wages or self-employment", "Yes, because unemployment compensation qualifies as earned income for EITC purposes", "No, because her investment income exceeds the $11,600 limitation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 32, the EITC requires earned income, defined as wages, salaries, tips, or net self-employment income. Interest, rental income, and unemployment compensation are not earned income. Since Okafor has zero earned income, she is ineligible regardless of her AGI level. Wrong (A): Low AGI alone does not qualify a taxpayer — earned income is a mandatory requirement. Wrong (C): Unemployment compensation is specifically excluded from the definition of earned income under Section 32(c)(2). Wrong (D): While investment income can disqualify a taxpayer, the threshold issue is irrelevant here because she has no earned income at all.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — CTC citizenship/residency requirement
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Ahmad (single, AGI $85,000) claims his 10-year-old nephew as a dependent. The nephew is a citizen of Canada, lives with Ahmad in the U.S. for 10 months of the year, and has a valid Individual Taxpayer Identification Number (ITIN) but is not a U.S. citizen, national, or resident alien. What Child Tax Credit may Ahmad claim?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$2,000 Child Tax Credit", "$1,700 Additional Child Tax Credit", "$500 Other Dependent Credit", "$0 — no credit is available"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 24(h)(4), the $2,000 Child Tax Credit requires the child to be a U.S. citizen, U.S. national, or U.S. resident alien with a valid Social Security number. Ahmad's nephew does not meet this requirement. However, as a dependent with an ITIN, the nephew qualifies for the $500 Other Dependent Credit under Section 24(h)(4)(A). Wrong (A): The full CTC requires U.S. citizenship, nationality, or resident alien status with a valid SSN, which the nephew lacks. Wrong (B): The ACTC is the refundable portion of the CTC — since the nephew does not qualify for the CTC, the ACTC is also unavailable. Wrong (D): While the CTC is unavailable, the $500 Other Dependent Credit applies to dependents who do not meet CTC requirements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — General Business Credit carryback/forward
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Keane Corporation generates a $50,000 General Business Credit in 2026 but can only use $30,000 against its current-year tax liability. Under IRC Section 39, what happens to the $20,000 excess credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It is permanently lost", "It is refunded to the corporation", "It is converted to a net operating loss", "It may be carried back 1 year and then forward up to 20 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 39, unused General Business Credits may be carried back one year and forward up to 20 years. The taxpayer applies the excess credit to the earliest available year first. This prevents the permanent loss of credits that exceed the current year's limitation. Wrong (A): The General Business Credit is not lost — Section 39 provides generous carryback and carryforward provisions. Wrong (B): The General Business Credit is nonrefundable and cannot generate a cash refund. Wrong (C): Unused tax credits are not converted to net operating losses; they retain their character as credits during the carryover period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — Child/Dependent Care Credit earned income requirement for MFJ
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Harmon and Vega (MFJ) have a 4-year-old child in daycare costing $10,000 annually. Harmon earns $95,000 in wages. Vega has no earned income — she volunteers full-time at a nonprofit. Neither spouse is disabled or a full-time student. Under IRC Section 21, what is the couple's Child and Dependent Care Credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0", "$600", "$1,050", "$2,100"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 21(d)(1), on a joint return both spouses must have earned income for expenses to qualify. Since Vega has no earned income and is neither disabled nor a full-time student, the deemed earned income rules do not apply. The qualifying expense amount is limited to the lower-earning spouse's earned income ($0), producing a $0 credit. Wrong (B): This incorrectly applies a credit percentage to some expense amount, but no expenses qualify when one spouse has zero earned income. Wrong (C): This applies 21% to $5,000, but the earned income requirement eliminates all qualifying expenses. Wrong (D): This applies 21% to $10,000, ignoring both the $6,000 two-or-more cap and the earned income requirement.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Child/Dependent Care Credit expense limits ($3,000/$6,000)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 21, what is the maximum amount of qualifying expenses used to calculate the Child and Dependent Care Credit when a taxpayer has two or more qualifying individuals?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$3,000 per qualifying individual", "$6,000 total for all qualifying individuals", "$8,000 total for all qualifying individuals", "$10,000 total for all qualifying individuals"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 21(c), the maximum qualifying expenses are $3,000 for one qualifying individual or $6,000 for two or more qualifying individuals. The credit percentage (20% to 35% based on AGI) is then applied to this capped expense amount. Wrong (A): The $3,000 per-individual limit applies only when there is one qualifying individual; with two or more, the aggregate cap is $6,000. Wrong (C): The $8,000 figure does not correspond to any expense limit under Section 21. Wrong (D): The $10,000 figure is the expense limit for the Lifetime Learning Credit, not the Child and Dependent Care Credit.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Adoption Credit rules and special needs exception
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Wallace and Park (MFJ, MAGI $200,000) adopt a child with special needs from U.S. foster care in 2026. They incur $8,000 in qualified adoption expenses. The maximum adoption credit is $17,280 (indexed). A colleague tells them the credit is limited to their actual expenses. Under IRC Section 23, evaluate the colleague's advice and determine the correct credit amount.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$8,000 — the credit equals actual qualified expenses paid", "The colleague is correct — adoption credits never exceed expenses incurred", "$17,280 — special needs adoptions receive the full credit regardless of actual expenses", "$9,280 — the credit equals the maximum minus actual expenses"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 23(a)(3), for the adoption of a child with special needs who is a U.S. citizen or resident, the taxpayer is deemed to have paid qualified adoption expenses equal to the full maximum credit amount, regardless of actual expenses incurred. Wallace and Park receive the full $17,280 credit even though they only spent $8,000. The credit is nonrefundable but may be carried forward up to 5 years. Wrong (A): This applies the general rule for non-special-needs adoptions, where the credit is limited to actual expenses. Wrong (B): The colleague's advice is incorrect because the special needs exception overrides the actual expense limitation. Wrong (D): This calculation has no basis in the statute — the credit is not reduced by actual expenses paid.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — AMT standard deduction add-back
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 56, which adjustment must a taxpayer who claims the standard deduction make when computing Alternative Minimum Taxable Income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Subtract the standard deduction a second time", "Add nothing — the standard deduction is allowed for AMT", "Subtract the AMT exemption in place of the standard deduction", "Add back the full standard deduction to taxable income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(E), the standard deduction is not allowed for AMT purposes. A taxpayer who claims the standard deduction for regular tax must add it back when computing AMTI. The AMT exemption replaces both the standard deduction and personal exemptions in the AMT calculation. Wrong (A): Subtracting the standard deduction again would decrease AMTI, which is the opposite of the required adjustment. Wrong (B): The standard deduction is specifically disallowed for AMT purposes and must be added back. Wrong (C): While the AMT exemption does replace the standard deduction conceptually, the mechanical calculation requires adding back the standard deduction first, then subtracting the AMT exemption separately.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — LLC no enrollment intensity requirement
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Castillo, a working professional (single, MAGI $75,000), enrolls in a single graduate-level accounting course to maintain her CPA license. She pays $4,500 in tuition for the course and is not pursuing a degree. She has never claimed the AOTC. Determine the maximum education credit available to Castillo.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$900 Lifetime Learning Credit", "$2,000 Lifetime Learning Credit", "$2,500 American Opportunity Credit", "$0 because she does not qualify"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 25A(c), the Lifetime Learning Credit equals 20% of up to $10,000 in qualified tuition expenses. Castillo's $4,500 yields a $900 credit. The LLC has no half-time enrollment requirement and covers graduate courses and professional development. The AOTC is unavailable because it requires at least half-time enrollment in a degree program for the first four years of post-secondary education. Wrong (B): The $2,000 maximum LLC would require $10,000 in qualified expenses; Castillo's $4,500 produces only $900. Wrong (C): The $2,500 AOTC is unavailable because Castillo is not enrolled at least half-time in a degree program during the first four years of post-secondary education. Wrong (D): Castillo qualifies for the LLC, which has no enrollment intensity or degree-seeking requirement.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — EITC no-qualifying-child age requirement
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 32, what is the minimum age requirement for a taxpayer with no qualifying children to claim the Earned Income Tax Credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No minimum age", "Age 25", "Age 19", "Age 21"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 32(c)(1)(A)(ii), a taxpayer with no qualifying children must be at least age 25 but under age 65 at the end of the tax year to claim the EITC. This age restriction does not apply to taxpayers who have one or more qualifying children. Wrong (A): There is a minimum age — taxpayers under 25 without qualifying children are ineligible. Wrong (C): Age 19 is not the threshold; a taxpayer must be at least 25 to claim the EITC without qualifying children. Wrong (D): Age 21 is not the correct threshold under Section 32.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — FTC carryback 1 year / carryforward 10 years
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT', $EXPL$Navarro paid $22,000 in foreign taxes in 2025 but her Section 904 limitation allowed only $15,000, leaving $7,000 in excess credits. In 2026, her foreign tax limitation exceeds her current-year foreign taxes by $4,000. She wants to use as much of the 2025 excess as possible. Under IRC Section 904(c), how much of the 2025 excess credit can Navarro use in 2026, and what happens to the remainder?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$4,000 used in 2026; $3,000 carries forward up to 9 more years", "$7,000 fully used in 2026", "$4,000 used in 2026; $3,000 is permanently lost", "$0 — excess credits cannot be carried forward, only carried back"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 904(c), excess foreign tax credits may be carried back 1 year and forward 10 years. Navarro's 2026 limitation has $4,000 of unused capacity, so she absorbs $4,000 of the 2025 excess. The remaining $3,000 carries forward for up to 9 additional years (the original 10-year carryforward period minus the 1 year already used). Wrong (B): The excess credit usage is limited to the unused capacity in the limitation — Navarro cannot use more than $4,000 in 2026. Wrong (C): The remainder is not permanently lost; Section 904(c) provides a 10-year carryforward period. Wrong (D): Section 904(c) specifically allows both carryback (1 year) and carryforward (10 years) of excess foreign tax credits.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

